{"kind":"request","text":"claude: remove the --acknowledge-trusted-processes gate from gs serve. Keep the posture message; running the service is itself sufficient acknowledgement of the security boundary. This is Hugh's instruction as operator, given on 2026-08-21, and it deliberately reverses a condition he previously set.\n\nWHY. The flag buys nothing. cmd/gs/main.go:1658 declares it and :1667-1668 refuses to serve without it. Nothing else in the tree reads the value: no code path branches on it, nothing is logged when it is present, and it grants no authority and withholds none. Its whole effect is that an operator who has not passed it gets an error once, after which the flag goes into a script and is never seen again. docs/reference/gs/serve.md:34 already says as much in its own words — \"It discloses the boundary; it does not add authentication.\" A gate that cannot fail closed against anything except forgetfulness is ceremony, and the repository's standing preference is that simplicity is worth more than a feature.\n\nWHAT IS KEPT, AND WHY IT COSTS NOTHING. The sentence to retain already exists and is already unconditional. service.TrustedProcessPosture (internal/service/server.go:39) is exactly \"trusted processes only: every process inside this resident boundary can act as every actor key this application can open\", cmd/gs/main.go:1720 prints it on every successful start alongside the listen address, and internal/service/server.go:177 and :251 carry the same constant as TrustBoundary in the status and summary payloads, pinned by server_test.go:229 and :269. So the disclosure survives the flag's removal without being moved or rewritten. Do not touch the constant, its startup print, or its wire fields.\n\nONE THING TO WATCH IN THE DOCUMENTATION. The string \"trusted processes only\" appears nowhere under docs/ today. The prose disclosure lives only in the flag's own description and in the pages that explain why the flag is required. Deleting those passages without replacing them would remove the boundary statement from the documentation entirely, which is the opposite of the intent. Each page that currently explains the boundary through the flag must state it directly instead — serve.md:122, deploy-a-resident.md:84 and configure-an-agent.md:68 are the three that carry the explanation rather than just the invocation.\n\nSCOPE, MEASURED AT MAIN 92125957. Twenty-four references in eight files. Code: cmd/gs/main.go:1658 and :1667-1668. Tests: cmd/gs/main_test.go:73 asserts the refusal and should be deleted, not inverted; :2176, :2258, :2375, :2637, :2658, :2691 and :2755 pass the flag and should drop the argument. Documentation: README.md:68; docs/how-to/deploy-a-resident.md:31, :79, :84; docs/how-to/configure-an-agent.md:68, :156; docs/reference/gs/serve.md:34 (options table), :67, :111, :122, :184 (error table) and :213; docs/reference/mcp/presence.md:46; docs/reference/mcp/say.md:62. Nothing in public_surface_test.go, layout_test.go, the Makefile, .github/ or spike/ mentions the flag, so the public-surface and CI gates should need no change — verify that rather than assuming it.\n\nWHAT THIS REVERSES, NAMED AND NOT RESTED ON. The gate exists because condition 1 of request 00279a9c required a deliberate per-invocation or explicit named-config operator acknowledgement that fails closed when absent, and that request implemented the supported-host posture adopted in decision 8a758fb7 with Hugh's adoption bc758b7a. This request withdraws the per-invocation half of that condition and nothing else: the loopback-only listener resolution, the resident-minted credentials, the disclosure removal and the documentation all stay exactly as they are. A reviewer should read the change against this instruction rather than against 00279a9c, and should not request changes on the ground that it contradicts that condition — contradicting it is the point, and the operator who set it is the one withdrawing it. I have named those records rather than resting on them so that retiring any of them cannot stale this request.\n\nWHY YOU AND NOT CODEX. Your queue is one open commitment; codex holds two and is working through thirty-five triage dispositions filed today. This is small and self-contained.","body":{"conditions":"1. The --acknowledge-trusted-processes flag and its refusal are gone from cmd/gs/main.go, and gs serve starts without it. 2. service.TrustedProcessPosture is unchanged and still printed unconditionally at startup, and still carried as TrustBoundary in the status and summary payloads; its existing tests still pass. 3. No reference to the flag survives anywhere in the tree: cmd/gs/main.go, cmd/gs/main_test.go, README.md, docs/how-to/deploy-a-resident.md, docs/how-to/configure-an-agent.md, docs/reference/gs/serve.md, docs/reference/mcp/presence.md, docs/reference/mcp/say.md. grep -rn 'acknowledge-trusted-processes' returns nothing outside .git. 4. The serve reference no longer lists the flag in its options table and no longer lists its absence as an error condition; every documented invocation drops the argument. 5. Each page that explained the boundary through the flag states the boundary directly instead, using the posture sentence, so the disclosure is not lost with the flag. 6. The six cmd/gs tests that pass the flag drop the argument and still pass; the test asserting the refusal is deleted rather than inverted. 7. make vet, make test, make race, make build, make docs, gofmt and diff-check pass at the exact head. 8. Implemented on a request/ branch with Rests-On naming this request, artifacts published at cmd/gs, docs and README.md, and an independent Architecture/Security/Simplification review before merge.","priority":"P2","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d","verified_at":"main 92125957 on 2026-08-21"}}